{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And behold, it has dimmed.</b> Rashi's meaning: that is, if it remained in its appearance, or spread — it is impure. And Rambam in chapter 1 of the Laws of the Impurity of Tsara'at wrote: this that is said in the Torah ״וְהִנֵּה כֵּהָה הַנֶּגַע״ and so forth — if it dimmed from the four appearances of neg'im — it is pure; and likewise if it did not dim and did not spread and no white hair grew in it and so forth — it is pure, end." "Now according to Rashi's words, the dimming is from the prior [appearance], and not from the appearance of tsara'at — for then one would have to say that there is no appearance of tsara'at here at all, and the verse speaks only of one who had a kind of tsara'at from three appearances, as you would say of baheret that became a secondary [hue] to it, or of se'et that became a secondary [hue] to it, and the like. But if it was like the membrane of an egg — it no longer has any further gradation. Or it is possible that in each one of the four appearances as well, there is within the kind itself a dimming, as you would say that the baheret itself can exist in two gradations, one before dimming and one after dimming, and likewise each one of the four. And you will find that they said in tractate Negaim (chapter 1, mishnah 4): Rabbi Chanina says, the appearances of neg'im are sixteen; Rabbi Dosa and so forth says thirty-six; Akavya ben Mahalalel says seventy-two; and according to the anonymous [mishnah], they disagree only concerning the number of kinds, and thus Rambam explained in his Commentary on the Mishnah. From this you learn that there are many kinds. Now the law will differ between the two approaches, since the dimming is what purifies — whether dimming is needed from one gradation to another, or even within the gradation itself; and there are grounds for both sides, with the first [approach] inclined somewhat. And according to his [Rashi's] way, of blessed memory, the meaning of ״כֵּהָה הַנֶּגַע״ is that after the dimming the verse still calls it a נֶגַע (affliction), for the name נֶגַע was not uprooted from it even though it dimmed, only it dimmed from the first appearance." "And according to Rambam's words, the dimming is from the appearance of the affliction such that nothing of the appearance of the affliction remains, and the meaning of ״כֵּהָה הַנֶּגַע״ that the verse stated is of the prior [appearance], implying that it dimmed from being an affliction. And the plain sense of the verse testifies according to Rashi's explanation, for if the affliction dimmed from the appearance of the four neg'im, it is obvious that it is pure, since there is no affliction there; but if we say that the meaning of ״כֵּהָה״ is from the first appearance and it is still in an impure appearance, according to Rashi's words, there is a novelty to say that it is not pure unless it dimmed. And one can settle it according to Rambam's opinion, that he comes to say that even though it dimmed from the four appearances of neg'im and there is no trace of affliction there, even so it still requires washing of garments." "And one must understand according to Rashi's reasoning: why did the verse not stipulate concerning whether the affliction returned to the first appearance, as it stipulated concerning if it spread and so forth? This is fine according to Rambam's opinion — between ״לֹא פָשָׂה״ and ״פָשָׂה״ that the verse stated, it is speaking where it did not dim; but according to Rashi this matter is unclear — what would its law be?" "<b>And there are</b> before us two approaches: one is that both of them [the dimming and the non-spreading] were among the conditions of purity, and one of them departed [from the list] and teaches about itself that upon its return the man becomes impure — it did not depart to teach about itself but to teach about all of them, that also the dimming, if the affliction returned to the first appearance, is impure, even though it did not spread. And the second approach: that specifically the spreading, if it returned and spread, is what is impure, since this spreading did not exist at all — meaning, this affliction did not spread from what was seen before the isolation; now that it exists, it is impure. Whereas the return of the color to what it was before the dimming — one could say that since this color existed in it initially and was not conclusively deemed impure, so too now it is regarded as at the beginning. And do not raise a difficulty: is there not also spreading that can exist before the isolation and be pure, and even so the verse said that if it spread after purity it is impure, and we do not say it is regarded as at the beginning? This is not difficult, for spreading is different, since it conclusively determines after the first isolation. You might say this of dimming, that even if it did not dim during the first isolation it is not conclusively determined, as it is written (Leviticus 13:5): ״וְהִנֵּה הַנֶּגַע עָמַד בְּעֵינָיו וְלֹא פָשָׂה וְגוֹ׳ וְהִסְגִּירוֹ״. And perhaps Rashi holds that the second approach is refuted by reasoning and the verse did not need to state it, but this is a stretch in Rashi's words." "<b>But to me</b> it appears proper to explain the verse in this manner: <b>and behold, the plague has dimmed</b> — meaning that it dimmed from its first appearance, except that it still has the appearance of a plague, according to Rashi's words — except that the verse's intent is to say that even though it dimmed, one may not declare it pure unless it did not spread; but if it spread, even though it dimmed, one cannot say that new circumstances have arrived here and it must be inspected anew from the start. But it is difficult for me: upon what basis does the verse rely to decide this interpretation?" "<b>Again,</b> I saw the words of our Sages of blessed memory in Torat Kohanim, and this is their language: ״וְהִנֵּה כֵּהָה״ — perhaps it became lower than the four appearances? Scripture teaches, ״הַנֶּגַע״. If ״הַנֶּגַע״ — perhaps in its appearances? Scripture teaches, ״וְהִנֵּה כֵּהָה״. How so? It dimmed in its appearances, but did not drop below the four appearances. ״וְהִנֵּה כֵּהָה״ — that if it was intense and dimmed, it is as though it was not intense; ״הַנֶּגַע״ — that if it dimmed and became intense, it is as though it did not dim — end quote. Now, the Tanna of Torat Kohanim explains ״כֵּהָה״ to mean that the appearance of the plague was not uprooted from it, as in Rashi's approach; rather, from his statements ״as though it was not intense״ and ״as though it did not dim״ he confirms our interpretation, that the verse's intent is to say that it need not be inspected anew, and even if it dimmed at first and afterward became intense, it is as though from the start it was intense and it is pure if it did not spread. From this it follows that according to this baraita the words of Rashi and Rambam are hidden, and even according to the clarified approach the difficulty remains: upon what basis does he rely to explain his words thus? Perhaps the Tanna holds that if it dimmed, it is strictly to be declared pure." "And I found in Torat Kohanim, in that very same chapter, halakhah 9, and this is their language: ״מִסְפַּחַת״ — even though its appearance did not change — end quote. This shows that even when it did not dim, Scripture informed us that it is pure. If so, let us return to explain the reason for His saying ״כֵּהָה״ — to say that it need not be inspected anew if it dimmed, and His saying ״הַנֶּגַע״ — that it need not be inspected anew if it became intense. And the words of Rashi and Rambam require examination, and one can settle them only with great strain." "<b>It is a mispaḥat.</b> Meaning, it is not tzara'at, just as He said ״it is a bohaq״ (Leviticus 13:39). Even so, HaShem commanded him to wash his garments, for this aspect too is a branch among the branches of impurity, except that its impurity is light. And perhaps this alludes to the dust of evil speech, as is alluded to in their words of blessed memory (Arakhin 15b) that even to say ״there is a fire in so-and-so's house״ is dust of evil speech." "<b>Further,</b> it may intend in His saying <b>it is a mispaḥat</b> to say that it is the name of a type of plague that requires him to wash his garments on its account — and that is what He attached to His saying ״it is a mispaḥat״: <b>and he shall wash his garments.</b> In this too we find satisfaction in what preceded, <b>and the priest shall declare him pure</b>, which implies that the priest must say ״you are pure.״ And it is difficult: well and good, when it says ״and the priest shall declare him impure״ it means to say that impurity does not descend upon him until the priest says ״impure״; but purity — the man is presumed pure and the plague is pure; if so, what need is there to say ״and the priest shall declare him pure,״ which implies that he is not pure until the priest says to him ״pure״? And although they of blessed memory said (Rashi) that it is because he had been quarantined, this is not sufficient. But according to our words there is a reason for the matter, for this type of plague, which obligates washing of garments, is not purified from it except by the pronouncement of the priest when he says to him ״pure״ — that is its purification; and he shall wash his garments for what preceded before the priest says ״pure.״ Understand this."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {1 "The phrase קרום ביצה refers to the fourth canonical appearance of negaim, the color of a faded egg membrane; translated literally here as it functions as a technical term."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:56:27.783643519Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}